was Duke of Macsó (1262–1272) and of Bosnia (1266/1271–1272); and thus he governed the southern provinces of the Kingdom of Hungary.", "newValue": "Béla of Macsó, born in 1243, was a pivotal figure in the southern marches of the Kingdom of Hungary during the tumultuous late Árpád period. As Duke of Macsó (1262–1272) and of Bosnia (1266/1271–1272), he inherited significant power and strategic territories from his father, Rostislav Mikhailovich, a Russian prince who had married into the Hungarian royal family. Béla's rule spanned a critical juncture, marked by the ongoing consolidation of Hungarian influence in the Balkans and internal struggles within the Árpád dynasty, notably the rivalry between King Béla IV and his son, Stephen V.

His tenure was characterized by efforts to maintain stability and authority over these often-rebellious frontier provinces, which included Macsó, Bosnia, Usora, and Só. Béla's position was complicated by his familial ties; he was a grandson of King Béla IV through his mother, Anna of Hungary, and thus intimately involved in Hungarian court politics. He initially sided with his grandfather, Béla IV, during the civil war against his uncle, Stephen V. After the death of Béla IV in 1270, Béla of Macsó swore fealty to Stephen V, though his loyalty remained a point of contention and strategic maneuvering for the Hungarian crown in its efforts to control the southern territories.

Béla's untimely death on November 1, 1272, at the Battle of Hód-tó, fighting alongside Stephen V against rebellious nobles, proved to be a significant turning point for the region. His demise left his duchies without a strong, direct heir, plunging Macsó and Bosnia into further instability. This power vacuum was quickly exploited by various Hungarian magnates, particularly the Ugrin Csák family, who vied for control, effectively fragmenting the once cohesive frontier administration. The subsequent decades saw a weakening of central Hungarian authority in the Balkans, contributing to the eventual rise of independent local powers and the Ottoman expansion in later centuries."}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-19T07:23:56Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#32227226033", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1243, http://www.wikidata.org/.well-known/genid/c8b52b6e0123f4a4fa84a26652c78fc2"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": "1243, http://www.wikidata.org/.well-known/genid/c8b52b6e0123f4a4fa84a26652c78fc2"}], "newValue": ["The marriage of his father, Rostislav Mikhailovich, to Anna of Hungary provided the familial and political basis for his claim to Hungarian ducal territories.", "The ongoing internal conflicts within the Árpád dynasty, particularly between King Béla IV and his son Stephen V, created opportunities and challenges for regional rulers like Béla of Macsó.", "The strategic importance of the southern Hungarian marches as a buffer against external threats and a source of resources necessitated strong ducal leadership."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-19T07:23:56Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#32227226033", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1272-11-01"},