figure in the tumultuous political landscape of 13th-century Central Europe, serving as Duke of Macsó (1262–1272) and Bosnia (1266/1271–1272). A grandson of King Béla IV of Hungary and a member of the powerful Árpád dynasty, his rule over the strategically vital southern marchlands of the Kingdom of Hungary positioned him at the nexus of Hungarian royal power, Balkan influence, and emerging regional autonomy. His brief but impactful career reflected the fragmentation and internal power struggles that characterized the late Árpád era, particularly the ongoing tensions between the monarchy and powerful magnates.

Béla's ducal authority extended over the Banate of Macsó, encompassing parts of modern-day Serbia, and the Banate of Bosnia, a frontier region frequently contested with Serbian and Bulgarian powers. His political career was largely shaped by his loyalty to his grandfather, King Béla IV, and later by his involvement in the civil war between Béla IV and his father, Stephen V. He was instrumental in defending the southern borders against incursions from the Second Bulgarian Empire and the Golden Horde, reinforcing Hungarian suzerainty in a volatile region. His marriage to Kunigunda of Brandenburg further solidified his connections within the broader European aristocracy.

His untimely death in the Battle of Macsó on November 1, 1272, marked a significant turning point, plunging the southern provinces into further instability and accelerating the decline of central royal authority in Hungary. The power vacuum created by his demise allowed local magnates, particularly the Ugrin Csák clan, to consolidate their control, ultimately contributing to the decentralization that plagued the last decades of the Árpád dynasty. His legacy underscores the fragility of royal power and the enduring strategic importance of the Balkan marches to the Hungarian crown."}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-19T09:15:38Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#32236501140", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1243, http://www.wikidata.org/.well-known/genid/c8b52b6e0123f4a4fa84a26652c78fc2"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": "1243, http://www.wikidata.org/.well-known/genid/c8b52b6e0123f4a4fa84a26652c78fc2"}], "newValue": ["The ongoing struggle between King Béla IV and his son, Stephen V, for control of the Hungarian Kingdom directly led to Béla of Macsó’s prominent ducal appointments.", "The strategic importance of the southern Hungarian marchlands, constantly threatened by Balkan powers and Mongol incursions, necessitated strong ducal leadership.", "Béla of Macsó's direct lineage from the Árpád dynasty provided him with the legitimate claim and support necessary to govern significant territories."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-19T09:15:38Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#32236501140", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1272-11-01"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement